  "query_preview": "..As the upper level low associated with Sunday's cold\nfront shifts into the High Plains, the weakening boundary remains\nin place across southern Utah. Since the front did not clear all\nthe way to Arizona, deep moisture remains south of the\nfront...with implications for the forecast for Tuesday.\n\nThis deep moisture combined with a subtle speed max embedded in\nthe southern jet will favor development of showers and\nthunderstorms after about 09-12Z Tuesday...mainly south of the\nremnant front. Looking at the 12Z HREF, similar to the 00Z HREF,\nthe 6 hour LPMM indicates a few locations with maxima over 1 \ninch, including ares near Zion National Park, the upper Paria \nRiver drainages over to Capitol Reef National Park and drainages \nin Glen Canyon Recreation Area. While this scenario falls short of\nthe confidence to issue a Flood Watch at this time, an upgrade to\na slight risk for excessive rainfall is forthcoming. Many of \nUtah's National Park and Recreation areas will be in \"Probables\" \nfor the Flash Flood Potential Rating for Tuesday.\n\nConvection may continue into the overnight hours Tuesday night\ngiven broad synoptic forcing. \n\nDeep moisture will remain in place through much of the rest of the \nweek as a strong upper level low shifts down the Pacific Coast. As\nsuch, the threat of flash flooding will persist, with showers and\nthunderstorms capable of rainfall rates in excess of 0.25-0.50 \ninches per hour through at least Friday, when the upper level low \nfinally ejects across the Great Basin/northern Intermountain \nRegion. Exact timing and location of heavier showers and \nthunderstorms will be somewhat dependent on ejecting shortwave \ntroughs in southwest flow ahead of the main low...and/or MCVs from\nprevious convection.",
